I stand waiting in line at the bank, scrolling through apps on my phone, thinking about disposable identities.
It isn’t a novel notion, I know, but it just feels relevant. Especially right now. I mean, look at me. Watch how I regulate my behavior, how I cultivate this one particular persona. Tapping my thumb against this image, this bit of text, providing low-key electronic support to friends and acquaintances while remaining painfully aware of my visible activity. Each interaction swivels a spotlight, letting everyone in my life know one more thing about me.
God, this feels stupid. Like, sure, I can’t think of a more ubiquitous social contract, but that doesn’t make it any less stupid. I could delete this account right now and I wouldn’t lose a fucking thing. I wouldn’t lose anything that makes me myself.
Think about it. Suppose I did delete this--no, all my accounts. Every last piece of my electronic footprint. That still wouldn’t stop me from burying my nose in my phone while waiting in line at the bank. It wouldn’t stop me from being that kind of person. Alright, so that pithy little description doesn’t qualify as my “real” identity either, but pretty much everybody ends up doing something like this during their painfully mundane routines. For fuck’s sake, I’m watching someone check their phone while interacting with a teller. They’re in the middle of a human interaction and they still need an escape.
I can imagine some disappointed grandparent telling me to find this person’s behavior disgusting, but honestly? I can’t blame ‘em.
Thoughts like these help pass the time, and I have no doubt that I’ll return to them later, but all at once the changing world around me renders them fucking useless. They dissipate utterly, cut through by crass, explosive words. The kind that almost never see the interior of a small town bank on a quiet summer morning. Words like fuck and up, and shut and hands and floor. Probably not in that order. I turn toward the sound, but the turn manifests as a whirl. Too fast. I stand disoriented for a moment.
Celebrities?
Taylor Swift, flanked by two other men, stands just inside the entrance. She holds a rifle and yells in a startlingly male voice. The others step forward. Lebron James holds a single pistol. Ryan Gosling holds two. 
Oh, I get it.
The masks don’t even look that real.
I hear screams. Some belong to me. Everyone moves, backing away. Or at least I back away. Holy fuck do I back away. I don’t get far, bumping up against the teller station. Should I climb over? No. Get smaller. I kneel. I hope to God that they want me to kneel because I can’t do anyfuckingthing else right now.
The robbers address the bank’s employees, demanding that they step forward. Robbers? What a stupid, juvenile word. Robbers. Fucking ridiculous. These men aren’t the bumbling scoundrels in an old-timey train heist film. These men could end my life on a whim. These men would probably find it funny to end my life on a whim. Taylor Swift moves her rifle and I can see down the barrel for the briefest of moments. My stomach turns. By some miracle I don’t vomit.
The door to the bathroom bursts open.
Our hero walks with certainty into the edifying glow of fluorescent lighting, brandishing his pistol like a prophet’s icon. Later I’ll probably think back and imagine him wearing a cowboy hat and matching boots, but for now I can find nothing special about him. He fires. The intruders move in unison, returning fire without reservation. Small craters appear in each opposite wall, visual confirmation of the kaleidoscopic aural onslaught. The moribund savior falls without a cry, like a tower of plastic cups. Inward tumbles his body and outward spills his contents.
Nothing moves, and somehow this paralyzation feels even more sudden than the preceding violence.
Then Ryan Gosling groans. Muffled by a pair of inanimate plastic lips, the noise nonetheless comes upon us as a tumult. It echoes like the bark of a starving dog, its audience transfixed not by volume but significance.
“Terry!”
Lebron James rushes over to Ryan--or, Terry--and as Lebron turns his head I see flashes of paler skin beneath his mask. Huh. Upon closer inspection, none of the disguises on display seem to suit their respective owners. Despite the criminals’ identical attire, black shirts and pants and boots and gloves, all of it distinctly not civilian, I can still make out their differing body types. The one named Terry, with his thick limbs and pronounced gut, missed his opportunity to pass for Ryan Gosling about a hundred and twenty pounds ago. Similarly, Taylor Swift’s broad shoulders and muscular arms contrast sharply with the delicate features of the mask he wears. As for Lebron James, not only is he obviously not black, but of the three criminals he easily has the slimmest body, his clothes billowing in waves as he moves.
“Shit! Terry, shit, shit, shit, are you okay?” Lebron exclaims, his voice cracking with the first curse.
“Watch the crowd, Joel,” Taylor commands, stepping toward us.
“You watch the fucking--shit. Shit.”
Lebron--or, Joel--moves toward Terry, who mumbles incoherently, his left hand reaching for his stomach.
“Whoa, Terry, hang on. Watch where you--”
Joel sidesteps, reaching forward to gingerly pull one of the firearms from Terry’s grasp.
“Joel!”, Taylor bellows, somehow even harsher than before.
“Shut up, Cardner!”
Again, Joel does not turn to face Taylor--or, Cardner--and instead reaches to help Terry, who stumbles through a clumsy attempt to sit. I honestly expect Terry’s other gun to discharge.
“Terry’s fucked,” Joel continues, finally looking toward Cardner. “We have to go.”
“Fuck you. Not without the money.”
“What?”
“Joel, we’re not going to leave without the money. There’s no fucking point in--”
“Fuck off.”
Joel wraps an arm around Terry, who shakes his head in protest as Joel hefts him back to his feet. Cardner rolls his eyes, comical beads of color behind the slits of his mask, animated cels in a dated cartoon. He turns to address me. Or all of us, I guess. The rest of us. The captives. Joel drags Terry toward the entrance, each party forsaking the other.
“Alright, fuckers.”
Cardner’s eyes sweep across the teller stations. They sweep back across us.
“See that?”
He gestures toward the corpse. I expect to see his hands shake. They don’t. I cannot even begin to imagine the depth of his sexual experience.
“That’ll be you, if I see any bullshit. So be good. Keep those fucking hands up, nice and high. We’re on a carousel. Everybody’s having fun.”
Blood rushes through me as Cardner steps forward. He turns as he walks, his rifle held as a baton before an orchestra. I swallow. It hurts, but only a little. 
“Cardner!”
Joel’s voice cracks a second time. Cardner stops but does not turn.
“Prince is gone.”
More silence, but a different kind. Panic, not shock. Ragged breathing fills the room. The baton has forgotten the fermata.
“What do you mean?”
Well that’s a stupid question. Even I know what it means. Everyone in the fucking lobby knows what it fucking means.
“Prince is gone,” Joel repeats automatically. “He took off. Adios. Arrivederci. Au revoir. Sayonara. How the fuck else do you want me to say it? He fucking left.”
One of the doors remains propped open by Joel’s foot. The bright morning sunlight pours inside, glinting off plastic and gunmetal alike. A single droplet of sweat slides down my right temple.
“The car is--”
“Yes, Cardner, the car is fucking gone. And Terry has a fucking bullet in his stomach. And you’re still worried about the money.”
More silence. Cardner stands immediately to my right. I look up. The mask curves to cover his chin. I cannot see beneath it.
“We take the money,” Cardner says, his voice monotonous, “And we leave Terry.”
Joel struggles against his burden. He shifts his weight, raising his gun. He points an accusation at Cardner, the most hostile silent treatment I’ve ever seen. No weapon has yet remained still for so long. Save for the dead man’s pistol, wherever it ended up. 
I stare, wishing I knew more about guns. Joel’s firearm defines the surrounding space with black, threatening angles. Stoic surety. It’s so fucking cool.
“Jesus, Joel. Fuck.”
Cardner throws up his hands.
“Fine. Close the door. Let’s figure this shit out.”
Joel lowers his arm and steps forward. Sunlight hurries back behind tinted glass as the door snaps shut. Terry grunts, fighting free from Joel’s grip. He slumps against the wall and slides slowly downward. I expect to see a smear of blood, but don’t.
Disregarding their injured partner for a moment, Joel and Cardner close the distance between them. They lean in, their hands rising and falling as they converse in hushed tones. They turn to us, silent in their analysis of our ranks.
I realize that I actually have no idea who else is in here, and an overwhelming desire to look around strikes me. I know that I shouldn’t move, of course. I know better than to draw attention to myself. No heroics. No cowardice. Is there a word for “cattle-like,” I wonder? Oh, right. Bovine.
But curiosity overpowers instinct. The room is quiet--no longer silent, but at least quiet--and even before I turn my head I feel profoundly thankful for the absence of wailing children. 
To my far left, in the waiting area past the teller stations, kneels a pair of Buddy Holly glasses. His trimmed beard is edged with meticulous precision. It stands out starkly against his pale skin, an indication of how little he has seen of the summer sun. For a moment it feels like our eyes meet, but I am left uncertain by the light reflecting off his eyewear.
To his right kneels a Resting Bitch Face, her mahogany hands fidgeting idly behind her head. Cheap tattoos litter her arms like spilled yard sale tchotchkes. I momentarily mistake her nicotine stains for an entry in this collage. Her hair, shaved short on the sides but otherwise long and full, tumbles down her back in a single heavy braid. Her gaze is locked onto the wall directly opposite.
Then there’s me, kneeling in front of the first teller station. I remember holding a check, before all this started. I have no fucking clue where it is.
To my right cowers an overweight Pinot Grigio. Her pink cardigan matches her peachy, flushed face. The shaking garment brushes against the tile, longing for the safety of a closet lined with “Live Laugh Love” accoutrements. She stares at the floor.
“Toss out your keys,” Cardner growls, interrupting my surveillance.
“Along the floor,” adds Joel. “Slide them along the floor. Take your keys out of your pockets--with one hand!--and slide them along the floor. If your hands do anything stupid I’ll just shoot them right off.”
“Hey, asshole,” calls a voice, as bitter as it is female. “Fuck you.”
I turn involuntarily toward the far right wall, my head rotating in unison with every other in the room. Except for Cardner’s. He continues to watch his captives.
“Are you kidding?” Joel exclaims, with genuine surprise in his voice.
“No, I’m not. Fuck you.”
The curse comes from a middle-aged black woman, the Manager of the bank. Her hair is big, her makeup simple and flawless.
“Well that’s good, because it wouldn’t have been a very funny joke. But that means you’re being rude, and I--”
“Shut up,” the Manager snarls. “You aren’t as cute as you think, and you’re not getting my car.”
“Lady, you obviously don’t understand. I am cute. And you, I’m sorry, but you’re stupid. And all of this,” Joel spins, anxiety rippling outward as his firearm charts a quick map of the crowd, “is true, because I have the gun. Ipso fucking facto, I make the rules. I make truth. And you are--”
“Here, Jesus.”
A clatter rises quickly. Cardner’s eyes widen. For a moment I hear death, but his gaze passes over me. The noise stops abruptly, a pink keychain now resting at the feet of the criminals.
“Take it,” says RBF.
I expect a quip. It comes after Joel picks up the keys.
“Really? You, a Lexus?”
“Joel,” scolds Cardner, “shut the fuck up. And you,” he turns back to RBF, “where’s it parked?”
“The parking lot out back. It’s black. License plate 575-JGD.”
“Thanks.”
I almost laugh. The gratitude of a rifle barrel, worth its weight in gold.
“Um, do you really think you’ll make it very far with a buncha masks and guns?” asks a white girl with a full head of Dreadlocks.
“Jesus, who cares?” Buddy Holly snaps. “Just let them go.”
“She’s right, dumbass,” Cardner growls, stepping forward. “Mrs. Lexus and I--”
“It’s Miss Lexus, asshole. I bought it myself.”
“Whatever. We’re going to get that car and pull around front. You two,” he points at the two tellers, a pair of Stilettos and a handsomely bald Wax Job, “are going to take Lebron to the back and give him all the large bills you got. Nothing smaller than a fifty.”
“We know his name,” Stilettos retorts. “It’s Joel.”
A glare from Cardner, as hard as the erection he must have for her. She stands tall, her pink heels augmenting her already considerable height. Sharp Mediterranean features compliment the soft abundance of her body.
Her coworker wears a disenchanted grimace, an expression spoiled by an air of practice. The contrast of this pretense against his buttery yellow button-down and heather gray vest creates a familiar, striking aesthetic. The skin of his pale, shaved head reflects the florescent lighting almost better than the laminated floor.
“Fifties and hundreds, got it?”
We do not learn if Stilettos understands. A noise, a voice, pushes through the walls, cutting off whatever response she’d meant to give. This voice is muffled like those of our captors, but by concrete and glass instead of plastic.
“This is the police. Come out with your hands up.”
The mechanically amplified words of God. 
Terry looks up from where he sits, white shock filling the slits in his mask. Joel and Cardner lock eyes. Cardner speaks first.
“How the fuck did they get here so fast?”
“I don’t--”
“How many are there?”
“How the--”
“Fucking look.”
“What do--”
“Kneel down, crack the door, and fucking look outside.”
Joel obeys. Cardner’s chest rises and falls, faster now than even my own. It’s gotta be hot inside that mask. Well, duh. It’s hot inside this whole fucking bank.
Sunlight flashes briefly and Joel stands again.
“There’s one car. Just one cop, too, unless there’s another inside the car.”
“D’you think we can make it?”
“Make it how?”
Their heads tilt in unison, their gazes angling to fix on each other’s weapons. Everyone emulates the man lying dead in the corner. Nobody breathes. Joel looks toward the entryway. Cardner looks toward us.
“Stay here,” Cardner barks, forcibly exchanging his rifle for one of Joel’s pistols.
Joel moves, assumedly to express the confusion we all share, but Cardner continues.
“I’m going to buy us some time.”
He strides forward. Taylor Swift smiles at me. Her unmoving countenance looms as a mockery of piety. A billboard ad over the face of the moon. She grins. I feel pressure on my bicep. The ground falls away, maybe forever. I soar into the air, up through the sky, vying to find the true visage of the Earth’s first satellite.
Except I am standing. Cardner’s hand grips my arm, squeezing and pulling. I turn to look, but at what? It doesn’t matter. The barrel of a gun presses up against the soft flesh beneath my chin. I find myself on my tiptoes. My hands splay wide. My will departs, escaping through the vacant white windows of my eyes.
Instructions. Loud, probably. I can’t really tell. I don’t even know the language in which they are spoken. But I know what they mean. I know what I must do, better than I know my own reflection. The muzzle beneath my jaw tells me everything, in simple and primal terms.
The pressure on my arm intensifies. We walk. The doors recede. The air is so thin. I realize that I can’t remember how to breathe. My mind lurches with fear. The doors are so far. We’ll never make it, not before I suffocate.
But we do make it. A crack opens in the space before me, a vertical line of brilliance. Light breaks upon me as a slow but unrelenting wave. I can see nothing else. My awareness is compressed, forced outward through an aperture that expands only through someone else’s exertion. Radiance blooms. I want to cry out as my vision overflows. I am born, delivered unto the world, blinking away tears that have not even begun to well.
I stand shrouded in white, deaf and dumb and blind, waiting to remember sensation. I feel the heavy summer air; the dull ache in my arm; the cool kiss of the metal lips holding aloft my head. I see a black and white car. A man stands behind it wearing brown skin and blue clothing. He holds something. A megaphone. No, a gun. Both? I can hear, too. The meaning of speech still eludes me, but I know the roar in my ear. The man standing behind the car roars in return. A subtle movement adjusts the gun pressed against my chin. Instinct adjusts my head.
I notice a boy across the street. He looks so young. His stands with hands raised, his bent little fingers cradling bright plastic.
What is this gesture? I know this. I know this. Tell me your secrets, little boy. Welcome me into your ritual.
My brow furrows as I stare. As I concentrate. A whetstone sharpens the diffuse luminescence clouding my sight. Suddenly, I can see everything. Reflections of a cop car in all these windowed storefronts. Wrinkles encircling the soft eyes of a policeman. Ants struggling to navigate the plunging irregularities of brick and mortar. The rolling miasma of heat emanating from every surface beneath the sky. The individual atomic rays of the sun. A boy and his phone.
Of course.
Dreams burst like soda bubbles, sweet and effervescent. Meandering years melt away. Nothing that came before this moment mattered at all.
Someone is recording me.
I am a hostage. Diminished, objectified, the full extent of my weakness on display for any rapacious audience. The corrosive degradation leaves my soul not just bare but prostrate, a private abasement captured forever by an unblinking lens. A low vibration in the back of my mind commands me to shrink from this humiliation, but I don’t. I can’t, because I find no humiliation from which to cower. I don’t feel disgraced by the exposure of my boundless human fragility. I feel liberated. Flung wide open, I am clean and honest. Too empty for shame. I smile. I grin. I accept this truth into my deepest heart, consenting and eager. My life no longer belongs to me. I am a vessel for the will of something greater. I have found my truest purpose.
I am jostled, spun. The communion ends without ceremony. I return to Earth. To the bank. This place has not changed, but I have. Everyone looks so different through these categorically altered eyes. They crouch, still stunned, and remain ignorant. Don’t worry! I’ll help all of you understand.
A crescent of cellphones decorates the floor of the bank. Joel walks among them, crushing each under the heel of his boot. The other captives glare at me. Cardner speaks, sweeping his gun across the room. For how long has it been away from my chin?
“The fuck is this?”
Cardner’s voice sounds different. I look over my shoulder. His mask is pulled up above his mouth. Of course. He needed the cop to hear him. His lips hang heavy and ripe, summer peaches abandoned by a decadent branch. The surrounding jawline is the blended beige of a suburban neighborhood. I see sweat. A lot of sweat.
He notices my stare.
“Fuck you,” Cardner grunts, tugging his mask back down. “Get the fuck over there.”
I ask where he wants me to go. My voice rasps in my throat. I can’t remember the last time that I spoke. Cardner inhales as if scorned and then he catches himself. It was a genuine question. He scans the room.
“Everyone, get in the corner. Away from the doors. Move.”
He waves his gun. We comply, fluid and organized. No one protests. No one moves to resist. The corpse looms in the corner like a shape in the road. Maybe it’s just cardboard, maybe it’s a dead dog. I keep it in my peripheral vision and the shape never solidifies.
Cardner addresses Joel once more.
“What the fuck is all this?”
“Loose lips sink ships, buddy.”
Cardner pauses, then says, exasperatedly,
“Do you really intend be here that long?”
Joel seems to speak but a different voice emerges.
“Hey …”
It’s Terry.
“Guys. My mom. I’d really like to talk to my mom.”
Joel kneels. He puts a hand on Terry’s shoulder.
“Hey, Terry. Don’t worry about that right now. You’re in bad shape. We need to get you out of here.”
“Joel,” Cardner intones, keeping his attention fixed on us, “ignore him. He’s wasting our time.”
“Jesus, Cardner, shut up. Just let me--”
“Fuck you,” Terry interjects. “Fuck you both. Just let me speak with my mom.”
Terry leans forward. He puts both his hands on the floor and, grunting, struggles to push himself to his feet. His gloves slip, tracing inscrutable red shapes like cave paintings. Terry stumbles and falls.
A phone rings somewhere behind the teller stations.
“I need to answer that,” the Manager says.
Cardner shakes his head.
“The fuck you do. It’s probably the police.”
“Dammit, Cardner,” Joel hisses, looking up and over his shoulder, “One of us should answer it. You should answer it.”
“Nobody’s going to answer that fucking phone.”
Joel stands, frustration and a curse escaping through a sigh.
“Watch Terry,” he says dismissively.
“Terry? I gotta watch these fucking--”
But Joel walks past Cardner, ignoring his protest, and vaults over a teller station. Lithe and angular, his narrow frame hangs in the air for just a moment longer than it should. He lands softly in spite of his heavy boots. The ubiquitous ringing guides him toward his destination. With his back to the room he lifts his mask and picks up the receiver.
“Yeah?”
“Hey,” Terry says, bringing attention to his presence once again, “give me a phone.”
“Shut up,” Cardner responds, without looking in Terry’s direction. “Joel broke the phones. You’ll have to wait.”
I consider this. Cardner is wrong. I was outside when Joel embarked on his crusade. My phone survived.
I call out to Terry, moving my hand toward my pocket. Cardner’s hand moves with mine and here I am again, staring down the barrel of a fucking gun. But it feels different this time. It feels less. There’s no more mystery. I’ve already shared myself with that inverted receptacle. It knows all my carnal secrets; it let me strip away its power. Now it’s just another hole. I pull out my phone, unlock it, and slide it across the floor.
“Thanks,” Terry croaks.
He fumbles for a moment. The phone falls with a clatter, now wet and dark. Terry peels off his gloves. Cardner bends, reaching toward Terry, and then stops. He looks toward us. Terry picks up the phone.
Joel speaks into the bank’s phone, “Hang on, Doughnuts, lemme count,” and looks toward us.
He is young, but not too young--about as young as he sounds, I think?--and Asian-American. His gentle eyes and boyish, ingratiating face clash with his willowy limbs. I imagine a Sistine Chapel cherub painted on the full height of a water tower, and it looks a lot like Joel. Round above, oblique below.
“Nine,” Joel says, concluding his calculation.
Terry tries to speak into my phone. He hasn’t lifted his mask, though I doubt that he cares any longer about concealing his identity.
“Hi, mom?”
“Not a chance, Doughnuts.”
“Mom, it’s Terry. I fucked up. Catastrophically.”
“Ooor--and stay with me here--or, I can hang up. How long do you want to keep playing this game? ‘Til the feds show up? Does that sound good? We can turn your small town bravado into a fucking joke.”
“No, mom. I’m not on drugs. Mom, I--mom, shut up. Please just listen.”
“Okay, good. Then you and I want the same thing, Doughnuts.”
“Fine, fine. You’re right. I’m genuinely sorry for interrupting. But will you stop for a second? This is more serious than--mom!”
“Goodwill? How about this, Doughnuts. My friend has been shot, and considering your line of work, I bet you’re pretty fucking savvy about that kinda thing, i.-fucking-e., you know that it sucks. Send in a doctor to look him over. Once we’ve got the doc, we’ll send out one of these precious little snowflakes.”
“Joel,” Cardner shouts, walking toward the teller stations.
“Mom, I’m hurt. Severely. I made a mistake and I’m really, really hurt. No, mom. Mom, will you--mom, will you cut it out?”
“Nope. An even exchange. One licensed medical professional for one stupid nobody.”
“Joel, we’re not going to send a single fucking hostage out that fucking door.”
“Fine, fine. I’m a pussy, okay? I acquiesce. I’m weak. But I love you. I love you, mom.”
“No shit. A brain like yours and you’re not a detective? Of course he’ll need to be relocated. But I’m not sending anyone out there until you give me something in return. A doctor, Doughnuts.”
Joel hangs up. He wrenches the phone off the desk and throws it against the wall.
“The fuck do you think you’re doing?” Cardner growls. “Don’t you fucking hold negotiations without me.”
Cardner points his gun at Joel, but the bluff is obvious. And kinda disappointing. Fucking commit.
“Mom …”
Terry ignores both his accomplices and stares vacantly at my phone. He sways slightly, his body tossed by gentle waves or nodding toward sleep. A noise comes from his throat. He reaches out and clumsily slides my phone back toward me.
I voice my thanks.
Muscle memory briefly takes control. My mind ignores my fingers as they tap out familiar patterns. The screen in my hands responds. Suddenly I’m staring into a mirror, or something like a mirror. I see what I look like as a hostage, held captive by a man in a Taylor Swift mask. Hashtag BadTaylor.
I call out to Joel and Cardner, interrupting their pissing contest to let them know that they’re trending. Joel narrows his eyes. Cardner cocks his head.
“The fuck?” Cardner asks.
I hold up my phone. Joel hefts himself back over the teller stations and leans in close. I find more depth in his soft eyes than I expected. His breath smells of whisky and something else, some sweetness I can’t identify. He instructs me to turn up the volume. I move to comply. Joel blinks, shakes his head, and snatches the phone from my hands. He restarts the video.
“Hey, don’t we get to see?”
I don’t recognize the voice. I look, finding that it belongs to a white Snapback. His pinstripe jersey hangs loose, draped like a canvas over heavily tattooed arms. How have I not noticed him already?
“What?” Joel asks.
“I just mean, if we’re gonna be famous, we should get to see.”
“Famous …”
The word lingers on Joel’s tongue. He rolls it around in his mouth, looking absently toward the doors. His eyes dim for a moment and then flicker back to brilliance. He returns his attention to the video. Cardner looms behind him now, watching over his shoulder.
“Yeah, you can see. You all can see. Everybody can see.”
Joel holds my phone up high. I move toward the back of the coalescing crowd. RBF and Snapback stand in front. Buddy Holly jostles between them, nudging Snapback to make room for Stilettos. Pinot Grigio moves in close behind but abstains from watching the video. Dreadlocks hops from one foot to the other, trying to see above Buddy’s shoulders. Wax Job sighs, leaning against the wall beside me. The Manager stands with arms crossed. Terry groans. The dead man does nothing.
The video plays for a minute or so. I can’t understand most of Cardner’s words, but his intent remains obvious. The officer speaks in the language of all officers, in clipped phrases and unapologetic demands. Dreadlocks looks back toward me twice, first when the camera brings into hazy focus the gun beneath my chin, and then again when my smile breaks wide open. It’s not exactly an iconic magazine cover pout, but it’ll do. The video ends after Cardner forces me back inside the bank.
Joel pockets my phone.
“This is perfect.”
He turns to Cardner.
“This is everything we need.”
Without explanation he hands the rifle back to Cardner, who in turn stuffs his handgun into his belt before taking the weapon. Joel tugs his mask back down. He crosses the lobby, staying near the wall. Once he reaches the doorway he lies flat, inching forward to peer through the glass.
“Holy shit, Cardner. This really is perfect.”
“How is any of this perfect?”
“There’s already a crowd. Two news vans. Three, maybe four cop cars. That’s a fucking cordon. They’ve even got dogs!”
“Ugh,” Dreadlocks blurts, rolling her eyes. “Pit bulls. Of course.”
Snapback scoffs.
“Seriously? Police hardly ever train pit bulls. And even if they did, so what?”
Dreadlocks frowns.
“Um, duh? Of course the police would use pit bulls. They’re basically the most violent dogs ever.”
Snapback chuckles. Then his expression falls.
“Wait, you serious.”
He shakes his head once.
“Here, it’s like this. Pit bulls ain’t even easy to train as guard dogs, let alone police dogs. They too friendly. It’s just instinct. They nanny dogs.”
“Nanny dogs?”
“That’s right. They used to be called nanny dogs ‘cause you could let ‘em take care of yo’ kids. Tch. ‘Most violent dogs.’ What even is that? Read a book, girl. Get off that David Wolfe juice.”
“Shut up,” Cardner snarls. “And Joel, I’m fucking tired of asking this question. What the fuck are you doing?”
Joel has returned. He reaches toward Cardner.
“I’ll need the rifle. And one of the scrubs. Uhh …”
He scans the crowd and I know that his eyes won’t settle on me. He pauses. I try to catch Joel’s attention. I tilt my head. Flick my eyes. Lean my body toward my recommendation. Joel reaches out a hand and points at Buddy Holly. Not my choice.
“That one.”
“You’re not going out there,” Cardner commands.
“And you’re not my fucking dad,” Joel retorts.
“You’re going to get us killed, dumbass.”
“No, Cardner, I won’t. Because we aren’t the bad guys. We’re the oppressed.”
Cardner makes a noise that suggests further argument, but he doesn’t follow through. Joel motions for Cardner to toss him the rifle. His partner grunts, hesitating, and then lobs it gently.
“You’re still a fucking dumbass.”
“I’m--”
But the Manager interrupts for a second time.
“Son, don’t you dare go out there like that.”
“Excuse me?”
Joel gestures for the crowd to part. We do.
“Don’t you go out there wearing that mask. Don’t you dare pull anymore shit wearing a black man’s face.”
“Lady.”
Joel taps his cheek. The mask tilts with an abrupt, disturbing rhythm, like broken animatronics.
“This mask is my entire identity. Everything I am, everything I could ever possibly be, is in this plastic mold. I can’t just take that off, can I?”
He turns to Buddy before she can reply.
“And you, look at me. The rifle is in my left hand, the handgun is in my right. Big Boomstick and Little Boomstick, respectively, of course. You and the Boomstick Brothers are going on a field trip, and teacher--that’s me--insists on using the buddy system. Little Boomstick is your buddy, and he’ll be right behind you the whole time. He pushes, you walk. He stops, you stop. It’s simple! Don’t worry about what Big Boomstick does. Follow your buddy’s lead and you shouldn’t have a problem. Disobey, and you’ll learn real quick: Your buddy likes to tattle.”
Buddy Holly sneers.
“Go ahead, asshole. Shoot me. Know what’ll happen? Literally every single cop out there will gun you down like rabid fucking dog.”
Joel nods.
“True! Very, very true. But hey, y’know what else will happen? If I shoot you?”
“I--”
“You’ll be dead, pal. Stone. Cold. Dead.”
Buddy receives no time to consider this. Joel waggles his guns. Buddy steps forward and Joel shoves him even further.
“Lean out the door. Lean out and let them know we’re coming. I’m armed, but I won’t shoot.”
They stand together for a moment, Buddy with his back to Joel. We all watch. Joel lifts a foot--an incipient kick--just as Buddy leans forward to push open one of the doors. A murmured white noise filters into the room, dying even as it reaches my ears. The crowd must be fucking rapt.
“Hey!”
Buddy’s voice reverberates off the tension outside.
“Hey, we’re coming out. I guess he wants to talk to you. He’s got some guns, but he says he won’t shoot.”
And then, quieter,
“Please don’t shoot.”
They depart. The door clicks shut behind them. Cardner steps away, assuming once more his role as warden. The two walls marked by the earlier gunfire stare at each other across the lobby, mirrored constellations, or the acne-riddled faces of star-crossed teenage lovers.
I hear an officer address Joel.
“Are you ready to cooperate?”
Joel shouts an inaudible response. I seriously fucking hope that I won’t have to guess at everything he says.
“No. I can hear you just fine. That is enough.”
Joel’s next muted reply precedes a brief silence, broken moments later by a hurried commotion.
“Back up, back up! Just throw it to him. Back up and throw it.”
I can picture Joel’s enjoyment, and I can imagine my own in his place.
“There we go!”
Joel’s voice rings out at last, distorted in its marriage to the treble static of artificial amplification. Hah. They gave him a megaphone.
“Now we can speak as equals. Well, almost. You have, haha, way more guns than I do.”
“You aren’t wrong,” the officer replies. “We have this entire block surrounded. Look here. And there. And up there. You can probably imagine what it looks like behind the building, too. Resistance won’t get you anywhere, I can promise you that. You won’t escape, and your charges will only get worse the longer we’re here. If you let everyone go, though--and let them go safely--maybe we can strike a deal. You might only serve five years. Less, if your cooperation doesn’t slacken.”
“Is this really how you want to start, Doughnuts?”
I catch Joel reusing that epithet, and wonder briefly whether or not this is the same policeman with whom he spoke earlier. I guess it doesn’t really matter. That title suits one just as well as another.
 “I came to you in good faith,” Joel continues, “I made myself vulnerable, and you’re going to throw my graciousness back in my face? Using a threat to open an amicable negotiation. You should be ashamed.”
“Nothing you are doing here is amicable, son.”
“Well what about you? Look at those guns, Doughnuts! I mean, you literally just gave me a lecture on how much firepower you have. Don’t do the whole hypocrite thing, please. You’re better than that. Let’s just be honest with each other, okay?”
A long pause follows. Even I have a hard time parsing that anti-logic. By the time the officer responds, he’s changed tactics altogether. He doesn’t fare much better.
“Son, what’s your name?”
“Lebron.”
“You don’t really expect me to--”
“Call. Me. Lebron.”
The officer sighs into his megaphone.
“Fine. Lebron. Listen to me. Those hostages in there, they’re innocent. They haven’t done anything wrong. They’re good people, and they don’t deserve to be hurt.”
“Good people? Are you kidding me? How the fuck do you know? You have no idea who’s in there. One of ‘em could be an axe murderer. Or a child molestor. Or a terrorist! Baaad people, Doughnuts. Worse than us! And hey, based on your logic, that means it’s okay to hurt them. Since they’re bad. Right?”
“Have you hurt anyone, son? We received multiple reports of gunfire.”
“Well that was a transparent deflection. But whatever, I’ll bite. How’s this? I promise that everyone inside is either one hundred percent okie dokie, or they’ve taken a bullet. My friend has taken a bullet. So where’s my doctor, Doughnuts?”
“That’s not what we’re talking about right now.”
“Right, of course not. Because that’s just one less criminal to worry about. I bet you want him dead, huh? You’ve probably got a half chub going right now, just from thinking about it.”
“That’s not--”
“No, that’s exactly what it is. With you. With all of you. You put on that uniform and suddenly you want everyone different to die. Tch. I cannot wait to see the social campaign they’re gonna launch against you. Or, I guess, I wish I could see it. But I’ll be dead. It’s coming, though. I can promise you that. They will bring you down, once you kill me. So what are you waiting for? Fucking do it. Give the order. Pull the trigger. Leave me in the fucking street.”
“Son, I don’t--”
“Do it! Stop living a lie! Show me what you are! Ferguson! Plaster me against the sidewalk! Ferguson! Ferguson! Ferguson!”
He continues and other voices join, the swelling chant quickly overwhelming Joel’s individual volume. Any other spoken words are lost, obscured and consumed by the trisyllabic hymn. The distance between declarations closes. That single statement fills the air like a cattle drive roar, a toneless bellow pushing relentlessly onward beneath wide, thunderous skies. It thrums in my bones, in all the Earth beneath my feet, maybe in every home across the nation. A single, infinite word, losing purpose and gaining meaning as the crowd screams louder and louder. An outraged summons for rain.
A door, thrown open, grants passage to that voluminous sound.
Joel and Buddy Holly back into the lobby. Joel holds a megaphone in his left hand. The handgun in his right presses against Buddy’s back. Buddy holds the rifle almost at arm’s length, his fingers nowhere near the trigger.
Carder and the Manager bark and growl in the exact same instant.
“What the fuck?”
“I told you--”
“A means to an end, my friends,” Joel replies, looking between them both. “A means to an end. Things are looking up. And you.”
He nods at Buddy, discarding the megaphone and retrieving the rifle.
“Nice job out there. Now get the fuck back in the corner.”
“You could’ve told me you were going to give me the gun,” Buddy laments.
Joel laughs.
“No way, José. Now scoot.”
Buddy obeys. Snapback smacks the back of his head.
“C’mon, man. You had a gun. What happened? Why you back in here, huh?”
“I …”
Buddy stops. He doesn’t look down but his eyes meet no one else’s. He chews the inside of his lip. Nothing emasculates like cowardice. I marvel at the ingenuity of it. What a clever way to forestall any potential uprising. Two birds, one brilliant stone.
Pinot Grigio speaks for the first time today.
“I prayed for you.”
She reaches out to touch Buddy’s arm, her other hand held close against her chest. Buddy jerks away.
“Oh you did? Good fucking job, lady. That’s so nice of you. I bet it felt absolutely wonderful, asking your imaginary friend to come save me. What did you expect? Did you think the ghost in the sky would take a break from ignoring all the starving kids across the world in order to keep me safe? Jesus fucking Christ, don’t congratulate yourself, lady. You didn’t so shit. Not a goddamn thing. You and your lazy … Fuck, I can’t even … That’s the problem, you know. With all of you. You don’t solve anything on your own. You pretend to care, but you never actually do anything.”
Stilettos sighs, rolling her eyes. She steps in front of Buddy, folding her arms and interrupting Pinot Grigio’s response.
“You do know that’s not the point, right?”
As she speaks, her tone crosses from patronizing to condescending. Buddy’s expression changes with it; shock briefly gives way to hurt, before disgust settles in to stay.
“Of course that’s the point. ‘I’ll just let zombie Jesus take care of it, I don’t have to lift a finger!’ It’s garbage, all of it. A bullshit fairytale excuse.”
Stilettos shakes her head.
“You’ve never actually been to church, have you? The Christian metaphor isn’t this ironclad boogeyman rhetoric you’re describing. It’s exactly what I said. A metaphor. A lesson. It’s a set of guidelines that help people act on their compassion. It’s about ‘doing the right thing.’ And guess what? Nobody knows what that ‘right thing’ is, not all the time. You sure don’t. No, please, don’t tell me you’ve never had to think about how to be the best version of yourself. And you know what? That’s what prayer is. It’s a call to action, not a self-gratifying dismissal of it.”
“Compassion in action? Ha. Yeah, there’s sooo much compassion in denying basic human rights to gays. In shooting up abortion clinics. I definitely remember the episode of Mister Rogers’ Neighborhood where he torched a puppet mosque.”
“That’s still not the point, though. You are right, yes, those are awful things, but the way you’re talking, you made it sound as if she,” Stilettos points to Pinot Grigio, “was doing that kind of shit right now. But she wasn’t! I don’t know, maybe that’s her weekend hobby, but right now she was just showing you a little kindness. If you do happen to see that kind of hypocrisy in action, please, by all means, call it out. I encourage it! I want you to! But if you’re smart enough to notice when that’s happening, you’re smart enough to notice when it isn’t. So back off, alright? She didn’t think that God would reach down and pluck you from the street. She was just doing the only thing that she could.”
Buddy blinks. He has no response, but frustrated voices continue to fill that empty space. Cardner and Joel argued straight through Stilettos’s monologue, and they don’t really seem interested in reconciling.
“That was different,” Cardner hisses. “I bought us time. And then you fucking squandered it by turning this into a goddamn circus.”
“Bought us time? Cardner, if you’d been able to look past the fucking dollar signs in front of your eyes we’d already be out of here.”
“The money was the whole point, Joel. And it was my plan anyway. My lead and my call.”
“First of all, get the fuck over yourself. It was Prince’s plan, remember? And even if it had been yours, so what? Shit’s gotta change sometime. Adaptability, bitch. Take some fucking constructive criticism. I mean, you straight up stole one of my lines this morning, so obviously my input’s good for something.”
“Seriously? This is your idea of adaptability, turning us into a reality show? If this goes sideways, Joel, I swear to God I’m going to fucking kill you.”
I doubt the sincerity of this threat, and I can see my skepticism mirrored in Joel’s eyes, but I don’t want him to follow that trite line of dialog any further. We’ve seen enough of Cardner’s bluffs. No, something else that Cardner said has grasped my interest instead. So I swallow hard and interject.
Carder turns like keys in an ignition, a rotating precursor to a controlled explosion. I ignore his ire and speak directly to Joel. They’ve already become a production, it’s true. There’s no point in ignoring it, no reason to pretend otherwise. So, hey, they might as well read the reviews.
Joel’s mask hides his mouth, but I can see the curve of a smile bending the corners of his eyes.
“See?” Joel says, still addressing Cardner. “This one gets it.”
He pulls my phone from his pocket and finds it locked. He curses, crossing the distance between us.
“Open it.”
I take the phone, unlock it, and graciously remove the lock altogether. I reach to hand it back and jump as the device’s ringing fills the lobby. Wait, what the fuck? Except, whew, no. It’s not mine. It’s the bank phone again. Joel hurries to answer it.
“Yeah?”
The word comes quick, almost before the receiver reaches his ear.
I look down toward my phone and I cannot fucking believe what I find. I mean, okay. It’s more complicated than that. I don’t find the commentary surprising at all, but I didn’t expect so much of it to appear so quickly. I’m holding hundreds, probably thousands of varying opinions on Joel’s sermon, and more of them manifest with every passing moment. Hashtag TeamLebron. Hashtag IStandWithBlue. Hashtag SecondAmendment. My thumb traces a familiar pattern as I read post after vehement post. Man, everyone loves this shit.
But I don’t have all day. I’m looking for something else, and I scroll on through.
“Not a chance, Doughnuts. I want the doctor first. Let me see the doc. There’s no exchange until Terry has to turn and cough.”
Hah! Paydirt. I smile at the other hostages and hold out my phone, acquiescing to the unspoken request, pressing play on the local news station’s recording of Joel’s preceding theatrics.
“Alright, alright. Fine. We’ll send some--shut up. I said it’s fine. We’ll send somebody out.”
Joel cups a hand over the phone, looking toward us. In one eye I watch the video, seeing Joel as he harangues the police. He stands tall, sneering out from under his hiked-up mask, genuine ferocity on his young lips. He reaches forward after each outburst, anointing every onlooker with the phantasmal phrases lingering in the throat of his hijacked megaphone. In my other eye I see Joel as he stands now, chest heaving, body tensed. He glances toward Terry. He points at Snapback and I press pause.
“Better ditch your phone for a burner, pal. You just won the lottery.”
Snapback puts up his hands, shaking his head.
“No way, man. I’m cool with whatever. I’ll stay. Let one of the ladies go.”
Joel laughs without mirth.
“See, that’s the problem. The ladies are supposed to go. But you? A white boy wannabe gangster? Nobody will consider your safety a victory.”
Snapback lets his arms fall.
“Man, I ain’t even white.”
Joel shrugs.
“Even better.”
Snapback recoils slightly, but the reaction feels simulated, like he already knew what his parents got him for Christmas. It’s not just that he finds this treatment familiar; it’s that he’s come to expect it. This disenchantment has done nothing to blunt his defiance, though, and he comments sharply on the coldness of Joel’s remark.
Joel shrugs again, returning his attention to the phone.
“Okay, somebody’s coming out. Yeah, yeah, he’s fucking fine.”
Joel raises his handgun. He points toward Snapback, and then points toward the door.
“Yep, right now. If he knows what’s good for him.”
Snapback follows the order. He turns at the last second, scratching under his chin.
“Sorry, guys. See you on the other side, I guess. Keep it real.”
He leaves.
I look down at my phone. I wonder how long it will take for his testament to pop up in my feed.
“That’s him,” Joel says, still watching the door. “I’m a man of my word, aren’t I? So don’t be a bitch. Give us that doctor. And Doughnuts, don’t try any bullshit. I shouldn’t have to say this, but I still don’t really trust you. So remember. If we see a gun, the hostages die. If we see more than one person, the hostages die. If we don’t see anyone at all, the hostages die.”
Joel kneels while he speaks. He holds the phone in the crook of his neck, his mask resting on top of his head. The rifle fills the valley of a teller station. Joel aims it toward the entrance. Cardner sees this and, taking the unspoken cue, steps forward to grab someone. RBF, as it turns out. He holds a pistol against her left temple. Her offended tch precipitates a monolithic silence, bereft of even the droning hum of a fan. Or an air conditioner. Ugh. It’s so fucking hot in here. The room smells of copper. Of blood. The dead man in the corner lies on his stomach, his head tilted to one side, his mouth gaping.
A shape looms outside. One of the doors swings outward, just a little.
“Goddamn,” Joel whispers, still loud enough for all of us to hear, “I love that new karma smell.”
The door opens wider. I can see the fleet of police cars beyond. The spire of a news van stretches like a metallic finger, hungrily reaching to push further inward. There can’t be only one. The rest undoubtedly lurk beyond the walls where I cannot see them. The crowd remains; an organic, mottled horizon. It probably hasn’t stopped growing.
The door opens completely and the Doctor steps forward from sunlight.
Joel stands.
“So you picked the short straw, huh?”
“No,” she replies, clear and unapologetic. “No one else was willing to come.”
She holds a first responder’s field kit by her side. Her eyes, alert and absent of fear, scan the room with measured consideration. She is likely of west or south Asian descent. Her bronze skin bears the records of age, deep lines adding texture to her face and steady hands. She blinks the longest blink that I have ever seen in my life. Then she addresses the room at large.
“That man is dead.”
“What?”
Joel raises his head, loosening his grip on the rifle. He studies the Doctor for a second before hopping over the teller station.
“Ohh. The guy by the bathroom? Haha, yeah, I know he’s dead. I don’t need you to check on him. You’re here to look at my friend. The guy sitting down, not the one lying down.”
“Joel, go check her bag,” Cardner warns.
“They’re both dead,” the Doctor replies.
“What?”
“Check her fucking bag.”
“Your friend, the one sitting down. I can see from here that he isn’t breathing.”
“Fuck.”
Joel’s mouth moves. His lips and jaw trace the expletive but the sound arrives mangled. He is at Terry’s side in an instant, kneeling, shedding his weapons and gloves and mask like a moulting insect. Cardner barks. He pushes RBF to the floor and backs toward Joel, aiming his gun at the Doctor.
“Nobody fucking move. Do not fucking move.”
The world shuts up.
“Drop your fucking bag, lady. Drop your fucking bag!”
The Doctor complies.
Joel’s searching hands molest Terry, touching his arms, his chest, lifting his mask and touching his cheeks. Dried blood paints Terry’s lips, a shade of red much darker than his beard. His eyes do not focus. Joel’s hands do not stop. They touch Terry’s neck, too quick to check for a pulse; Terry’s stomach, too quick to find the wound; Terry’s forehead, too quick to do anything more than leave behind a residual red handprint like fresh warpaint.
At last Joel pulls away. His hands trail through the air, wavering slightly, on the verge of casting magic. He slumps, for a moment remaining utterly still. Then he reaches once more, this time to grip Terry’s body and drag the bulk of it into his lap. Joel holds the corpse and sobs in silence. I stare. I feel like I’m reading his diary and I just can’t stop. He kneels on a bank floor like a theater stage, broadcasting his most private and treasured thoughts beneath unknown spotlights that blister his skin and burn away his armor. His body trembles, pushed and pulled by the churning pistons of grief. I wonder, enraptured. Maybe his body isn’t moving at all. Maybe the Earth is. Joel’s body is static and the Earth moves around him.
Every member of the audience watches through their own keyhole at this public spectacle of loss, but Joel does not shrink from the exposure. He does not close off his heart. He remains open. Honest and abject. Just like I did. Just like me. Fuck. My phone feels like a sword. Could I get away with recording this?
By gradual inches Joel pulls away. He holds Terry’s face in his hands. Terry’s lips pucker. His cheeks contort. His eyes stare a million miles past Joel. Cardner, still watching the room, puts a hand on Joel’s shoulder.
“I’m sorry.”
Joel looks up. He lets Terry’s head fall. He stands.
“You’re sorry.”
It’s not a question.
“I am. For my part.”
“For your part?”
Joel turns to face his hostages.
“Hey everybody! He’s sorry for his part. He’s sorry for his part.”
He turns back to Cardner.
“God. I’m so glad, buddy. I’m so fucking glad that you’re sorry. That makes me feel so much fucking better. What do you think your part was, huh? Worrying about the Doctor’s fanny pack?”
Joel storms toward the Doctor. She does not move. He grabs her bag from the floor and upends it. Most of the contents spill outward. Gauze, ointment. Joel holds the bag above his head. He reaches inward. Tweezers tumble out. Scissors. Thermometers. Nothing more. Joel stares for a second before throwing the bag. He balls his fists and kicks the assorted equipment, hard, sending some of it flying, some of it skidding across the floor.
“There, Cardner. At least one of us got what we wanted.”
“This isn’t--”
“Of course it’s not.”
“Joel. I’m sorry.”
“You wanted the money.”
Joel does not yell. His eyes, hard and red, pierce like bloodied spears. He plants his feet and grows to fill the room.
“You wanted to make sure that we got our dues. Everybody knows, Carder. Every fucking person in this fucking bank knows what you wanted. I know, best of all. And I know something else, too. I know that if you hadn’t been such a colossal fucking shitwhistle--if you had just let it fucking go, Cardner, for once in your goddamned fucking life--Terry wouldn’t be dead.”
Cardner lowers his weapon. Not a single person moves.
“Maybe.” 
A knife is honest. The blood in the cut is payment, not healing.
The Manager inhales and speaks.
“Both of you. Both of you did this.”
Joel’s face darkens, but she perseveres.
“Both of you are responsible for that boy’s death, and you know it.”
“Oh for fuck’s sake,” Buddy starts, but nearly everyone silences him with individual glares.
“You’re a team,” the Manager continues.
Joel slowly advances on her. 
“Whether or not you meant to, you all agreed to take equal responsibility for whatever happened. That’s what it means to be a team.”
Joel, at least half a foot taller than the Manager, towers above her. He leans down, hands on his hips, jaw clenched. The Manager straightens her back, squaring her own jaw in turn.
“When you’re part of a group, or part of a team, or whatever, you don’t get to choose which actions you want to stand by and which you want to renounce. Sure, you can speak about how you feel, but in the end, you’re in the ranks. You’re in the ranks and you carry the burden. Even if you want to do the right thing, and even if you succeed at it, anyone else can balance out what you’ve done. When you have a voice, you have power. Power to influence, to direct. Whether or not you want it, it’s yours. And if you’ve failed to use that power correctly, to keep your teammates from doing bad shit, well, that’s on you.”
Joel makes a noise, but the Manager continues.
“That includes making amends for anyone who fucked up in the past. You don’t get to be part of something without inheriting that legacy. Even if you didn’t join up on purpose, if you’re part of a community, everyone and everything in there is your responsibility.”
A strange smile quirks Joel’s lips.
“I don’t think you’re talking about me anymore,” he says quietly.
“Hey!” Dreadlocks chimes. “What do you mean, even if we didn’t join up on purpose? There’s no way I’m responsible for, like, other peoples’ stuff. Other women, or whatever. Other people in this room. It doesn’t matter. Nothing that anyone else does is my fault.”
“Yeah,” Buddy adds. “Exactly. That’s just, what the actual fuck? So I’m also responsible for that asshole’s death, just because I’m stuck in this bank? That’s literally fucking bullshit.”
Joel glances toward them.
“Shut up.”
He returns his attention to the Manager. His smile remains. He uncoils and offers a proclamation.
“She’s right.”
He looks toward Cardner.
“I did this too.”
Cardner raises a hand--the one not holding a gun--and for a moment I think that he’ll lift his mask. He seems to think so, too. But he doesn’t. He looks toward Terry’s body, and then back at Joel.
“I’m still sorry.”
Joel softens.
“I know.”
A pressure departs, the release of iron bonds. Somatic fraily returns. Joel’s ominous smile begins to wane and, shit, I see an opportunity fading. So much work remains unfinished. I need to step in. Their reconciliation can wait ‘til after. Or, fuck it, what the hell do I care? We have heavy work on our hands. Greatness demands sacrifice. 
These boys need direction, too. We stand at the center of a wild, unfathomable network of data, of digitized emotion. So much weight and value radiates out from this stuffy, sweltering room. Raw feeling courses through synapses and into wires, where it gets encoded into radio waves and flung through the air. Literally through the air. What we do here will pass through everyone, as long as we keep that data pouring outward. If they focus on each other? That network dies.
So I speak. I suggest that the truth of Terry’s story needs to be told. Cardner remains impassive. Joel’s nostrils flare before he speaks.
“What do you mean?”
I’m pretty sure that he already understands, but I play along. Terry is--well, was--a friend of Joel’s. That much, at least, everyone here can see. But that wasn’t all of him. Terry’s relationships, his associations, that stuff can’t capture the totality of his identity. He wasn’t an accessory. He was an individual. Inertia and agency, willpower and dreams. He had a story. So where did it go? Who now gets to say what Terry was and was not?
All the other hostages glower at me, I can feel it, like a thousand little lasers powered by indignation. I don’t care. In time they’ll come to understand the idols on my altar.
Joel looks toward the entrance, then toward the teller stations. He looks at Terry’s body, and Cardner, and me.
“Motherfucker,” he muses, rubbing his chin. “I guess we’re not done yet.”
Joel demands my phone. I deliver it willingly. He gives me a thin smile and chimes,
“The crusades weren’t fought in a day.”
“That’s not--” Wax Job begins, before cutting himself off.
Joel walks toward Terry’s body, patting Cardner’s shoulder as he passes. Cardner starts, steps forward, and points a large finger at the Doctor.
“Get with the rest.”
He jerks his thumb back toward us. He keeps his gun lowered.
“I figured,” the Doctor replies.
She steps past Joel, who kneels lightly beside Terry’s body, holding my phone above his head like a bent corona. He adjusts the extension of his arm and presses a thumb against the screen. He speaks slowly and quietly, his eyes staring deep into the iris of the device.
“Hey, this is Joel. But you know me as Lebron. The mask, remember? Hashtag TeamLebron? If you miss the mask, well, so do I. But I need to be honest with you for a second.”
He moves his hand, presumably to bring Terry’s body into view. He does not look away from the phone.
“My friend is dead. His name was Terrance Collins. This is why I was shouting at the police. And, well, I don’t know how all of this will end, but I definitely know that Terry is no longer in control of the part he’s playing. Intellectual property rights, revoked. History is written by the victors, right? And Terry definitely fucking lost. So I guess I’m here to fill in the blanks.
“Terry and I met in third grade. I hated him at first. I mean, not in any real way--I don’t think kids can really hate--but we sure as fuck weren’t fast friends. It wasn’t until middle school that I really got to know him. We were on the same little league team for a couple years. He didn’t play all that well, but he was still one of the best, y’know? He only hit the ball three times a season, but every single hit was a home run.
“He was like that in a lot of ways. Quiet but surprising. In high school he once punched a classmate who was harassing my girlfriend. I honestly thought that he was just trying to show me up. Trying to steal her. If that had been his goal? It probably would have worked. But no, he was just being a friend. Loyal and reliable.
“We went to different colleges and fell out of touch, because I guess that’s how friends grow up. He wanted to be a journalist. I think he tried for a while, as a freelancer, but the siren song of a rural hometown is stronger than the ambition of a generation. He ended up back here. We both did. He didn’t punch anyone for me this summer, but maybe that’s just because he was waiting to take that bullet for me.”
Joel pauses. He looks into Terry’s empty eyes. He closes them clumsily and his fingers remain. The stillness broadens into a moment of silence.  
“And now I’m giving his eulogy. Twenty-six. Twenty-fucking-six.”
He stands, now looking down toward the phone.
“The paramilitary pigs out there played a game with Terry’s life, and it looks like they fucking won. But also? Of course they did. Of course they fucking beat him. They’ve got more guns. They’ve got riot gear, gas grenades. They’ve got military surplus humvees. They’ve probably got a enough bullets out there to execute this entire town. One to a man.”
He pulls the phone in close.
“I’ve got a mask, sure, but what about them? What about that achromatic veil they wrap over their hearts? Blue, blue, blue, until they are one and the same, one mind and one body bent toward a singular purpose. They killed Terry, make no mistake. They wanted it, and they got it. That’s how power works. So what comes next? A blackout raid? Gas and chemicals? A drone strike? Who’s going to stop them, anyway? They have the power, they can do what they want. Nobody will challenge them because nobody can challenge them. That’s. How. Power. Works. They killed Terry, and they’re just going to keep on doing whatever the fuck they want. This must not go on! Terry is dead, and this must not go on!”
His hands shake as he posts the video. Veins bulge on his neck and his temples. He looks up, and I’m not sure what I see in that moment, before he closes his eyes and slows his breathing.
Cardner takes a step forward. He slouches, his shoulders slumped like landfill nightmares. His head seems almost to float with indecision. A wet varnish glazes his eyes, but it doesn’t rob them of their focus. He speaks, his tone marrying exhaustion and hostility.
“Joel, how much of that was true?”
Joel exhales a long, steady breath, his cheeks puffing out and then deflating.
“Enough of it. The right parts, at least.”
A phone rings. We all jump, a little. Joel and Cardner look toward the sound, and then back at each other, in perfect unison.
“Well that was fucking fast.”
Joel moves toward the teller stations and then stops, turning back slowly.
“No, that was too fast. This isn’t about the video.”
He stands, brow furrowed, and then sighs. His lips purse with chagrin.
“Oh sonofabitch. They know about,” Joel pauses to gesture toward the corner, “that. The body. John Wayne’s corpse. That’s what this is about.”
Cardner nods, offering no other input. Joel, after a brief glance toward the ceiling, snaps his fingers. He points through Cardner.
“That fucking thug, the one we let go. He fucking talked. Of course he fucking talked. Ugh.”
The phone continues ringing.
“So are you going to answer that?” Cardner asks at last.
“What? No. No fucking way. Need to give it some time, y’know? Let that video circulate, make a good impression.”
“Joel, seriously? I mean, look, I’m sorry about Terry. I really fucking am. But you don’t have a plan, and we really need one right now. These stunts are a distraction. And that’s at best. At worst, they’re going to get us killed. Really. You keep this shit up and we’re dead, just like Terry. We fucked this up, hardcore, and unless we pull our heads out of our asses and do something, we are fucking dead.”
Only now do I realize how deeply Terry’s death affected Carder, as I watch him try to reclaim his ebbed authority. Could I have succeeded at convincing Joel to post that video if Cardner hadn’t been subdued?
Joel shakes his head, chuckling lightly.
“Cardner, do you really think we’re going to get away? I mean, physically escape? Do you really think that there’s some way we could pull that off? ‘Cause I sure as shit don’t. We’re surrounded. Trapped, worse than a wife in a Scientologist marriage. We can’t get away, so I’m trying something else. Brains over brawn. The socially engineered pen is mightier. Here, let me show you. Just gimme a sec.”
Joel, holding a pistol in one hand and my phone in the other, directs his attention toward this latter tool. Cardner waits, realizes the depth of his partner’s distraction, and grudgingly moves about the room. He retrieves Terry’s second, now discarded firearm and tucks in into his belt. He picks up the rifle. He leans back against a wall, ignoring Joel and watching us silently.
My shirt clings to my body, stuck there by a layer of stale sweat. I wipe my forehead, and the space above my lips. This oppressive heat feels eternal. Will the sun never set again? Muted light radiates from behind the tinted doors, and I swear to God it doesn’t look any fucking different than it did this morning. How long have we been here? The clock on the opposite wall lies to me, I know it. I don’t necessarily want to get out, but the sun can feel free to fuck right the hell off.
Finally, Joel speaks.
“Holy shit. This is better than I expected.”
He looks toward Cardner and holds out the phone.
“Check this out.”
For a long minute Cardner says nothing. Then, quietly,
“That’s fucked up.”
“Right?!”
Joel pulls the phone back toward him, his eyes wide.
“And that’s not all. There are so fucking many! Listen to this one: ‘I’m hashtag TeamLebron, hastag TeamJoezy all the way! Hey Joel, once they let you out, you can come tie me up. Hashtag JoelsNextHostage.’ Or this one: ‘Joel, if you need target practice,’ eggplant emoji, water splash emoji, ‘I’ll dress up as your bad cop. Hashtag JoelsNextHostage.’ And there’s this meme--”
Joel’s exposition continues, but a snarl diverts my attention. I turn to see RBF rolling her eyes and speaking through gritted teeth.
“Fucking disgusting.”
The Manager shakes her head, an inverted sign of agreement.
“Those poor girls.”
Stilettos looks ponderously between both of them, and then shrugs.
“So what?”
The Manager reacts as if slapped, her eyes shooting open, more out of shock than pain. RBF balks, her nose flaring and her lips curling. She studies Stilettos for a long moment, narrowing her eyes.
“Wait, do I really need to explain this to you? ‘So what?’ Fucking hell, this is why I could never date a Jesus chick.”
Stilettos shrugs again.
“What’s wrong with women who express their desires? If they consent to it, there’s no harm in that.”
RBF snorts.
“Right. Consent. That’s definitely why I’m here.”
“Hey,” interjects Wax Job. “What proof do we have that all those statements are from girls, anyway?”
A variety of sudden stares does not provide an answer to his question.
“What? He’s not bad-looking.”
Joel’s volume suddenly rises, as if to acknowledge these contingent attentions.
“--and then there’s all these fucking offers to make videos with me. Jesus, Cardner.”
Fuck, that’s it! I know exactly what comes next. 
I raise my hand, interrupting Joel to ask permission to see my phone. I promise no foul play. I swear to it. I smile gently, inviting him to watch. Cardner wants none of this, expressing his malcontent through a litany of growled slurs. I ignore them all. Joel steps near and relinquishes the phone. I hold it down but away--so that only he can see--and open all my dating apps, logging out of each in turn. I don’t explain myself. I don’t need to. I return the device to Joel, freezing briefly when our gazes intersect. A thousand times could I catch his eyes and never once would I see the same person.
“What the fuck--” Cardner begins, before Joel quietly cuts him off.
“The fuck do you care?”
Joel doesn’t just sound dismissive. He sounds outright distracted. He barely looks up from the phone when he speaks, his legs carrying him away from me and, rather than back toward Cardner, into the center of the lobby. My heart swells. 
Cardner stands without speaking. Joel frowns his concentration down toward the device, his fingers tapping furiously against it. He looks up, and down, and up again. His gaze darts between the members of the assembled audience, between his partner and his hostages.
Cardner shakes his head.
“Joel, whatever dumbfuck thing you’re doing, knock it the hell off. We need to--”
“What?” Joel snaps, the venom in his tone honestly surprising. “We need to what, Cardner? Oh, that’s right, you don’t actually know. Well, since this was all your big fucking plan, figure it the fuck out for yourself. Until then, I’m going to keep doing what I need to do.”
“Give me the phone.”
The demand comes immediately, and I suspect that Cardner intended to say this even before Joel interrupted him. But then again, Cardner hasn’t even reached for the thing. Another moment passes and he still does not. Maybe he actually didn’t mean to say anything. Or at least, didn’t mean to say that. Maybe the words spilled out all on their own, pushed past his lips by stress and anger and fear, even though he knew that he needed to hold them back. Because now he’s testing Joel. He’s testing the loyalty of the only ally he has left.
Joel moves. Toward me. 
Hallefuckinglujah.
“Hey, I need you to take my picture.”
From the corner of my eye I see Cardner, paralyzed as if caught in a freeze frame, staring from behind the impenetrable wall of his mask. He tenses. I can practically hear the calculations ticking through his mind. Is the experience mutual? Can hear my own thoughts, too?
Hey, buddy, I’m doing this for you.
Suddenly Joel stands before me. I dimly sense Cardner stalking past to confront the rest of the hostages. Or maybe just the employees of the bank? I dunno, it doesn’t really matter. I have the phone in my hands again. Joel poses with his pistol and I snap a picture. And then another, and another, on and on. He scowls. He raises an eyebrow. He holds the gun out with one hand, resting the other against his hip. He pulls a face. He laughs. He aims the gun like a marksman, the sights pointing slightly above my left shoulder. He mimicks gunfire, pew pew. He straightens his back and holds the gun down, one hand over the other. He sets the gun on the floor, stuffs his hands in his pockets, and feigns a pensive expression.
Joel winks at me when we finish, smiling generously.
“Thanks a lot. Time to score some ten outta ten hotties.”
“Wait,” Cardner calls, his prior anger replaced by sudden and utter incredulity, “that’s what this is? You’re setting up a dating profile?”
His voice sounds flat, but not empty, like a bright, colorless light. Toneless amazement overrides his other emotions, but why? This is only the next logical step.
“Profiles,” Joel corrects, placing special emphasis on the plurality of the noun.
“Well, what about Cassandra?”
Now it’s Joel’s turn to suffer that same nullifying amazement. He pauses, looks around the room, and cocks his head to both sides. He speaks frankly.
“Are you serious?”
“I just mean that this’ll probably upset her. In a more personal way than,” Cardner gestures at the room in general, “all of this. You’re going to hurt her, Joel.”
Cardner moves toward Joel, and a murmured conversation snags my attention before I can think of how to intervene.
“What a dog,” Dreadlocks mutters, nearly sneering.
“Right?” Buddy adds. “Cheating is fucking gross. Cheaters are fucking gross.”
“Really?”
The dissent belongs to Wax Job. He looks between Dreadlocks and Buddy, but no reply is forthcoming. He presses on.
“Okay, fine. In a way, you are correct. But the scope of that statement seems a bit too broad. ‘Cheating is gross, so all cheaters must be gross.’ The underlying notion, though not explicitly delineated, is implied well enough: It is disgusting to let base desires override a bond of trust. Except that there are subtler things at work. Things more nuanced than the biological libido.”
Buddy raises both eyebrows. He gestures vaguely behind him before responding.
“Oh yeah? What about the two goddamn corpses in this fucking bank? You sure you want to keep talking about subtlety?”
“A fair observation, but one unrelated to the point at hand. I’m talking about cheaters. About the vagaries of human desire, and how difficult it can be to reconcile desire with obligation. Is it so terrible to take in secret something that we know we need? I posit that it is possible to love, value, and care for a partner while finding, through any means necessary, the things that they cannot provide in turn.”
“Good job. Keep trying to justify all the shitty things you’ve done in your own relationships.”
Wax Job closes his eyes and pinches the bridge of his nose, inhaling and exhaling with deliberate consideration.
“Ad hominem attacks will win you nothing. I’m just trying to say that a pain unfelt is not really a pain at all. An act of betrayal that yields no consequence exists forever as a hypothetical. And punishing a supposition is unjust. We deserve what we want, and if we can get it without hurting the people for whom we care? There is no harm in that. The heart is labyrinthine. Desire is both Theseus and the snorting Minotaur.”
“That’s--”
“I’m pretty sure,” Dreadlocks retorts, speaking over Buddy, her words sharp and direct, “that this won’t go unfelt by Joel’s girlfriend.”
Wax Job blinks, and then chuckles wryly.
“You make a good point.”
“I’m not so sure,” Stilettos concedes, adding her voice to the discussion. “Not about Joel’s girlfriend, but about dismissing this argument. I think there’s something here, in a way. Love is complicated.”
Buddy rolls his eyes.
“Of course you do. Of fucking course. Ugh. Girls.”
He turns to Wax Job.
“Nice job, baldo. Well done. Looks like you found somebody to bang, if we actually live through this shitshow.”
Stilettos opens her mouth, smoldering, but Wax Job’s honest laugh cuts her off.
“Sweetheart--and I mean absolutely no offense by this--even if I did expect to seduce someone so easily, you would never be the target of my predation. You lack the … requisite anatomy.”
Confused creases form on Buddy’s brow.
Joel and Cardner’s conversation continues past this sudden denouement, but only for another sentence or so, until the phone interrupts them once again. It rings louder than ever before, filling more frequencies with a screeching treble and a rumbling bass. It cuts through speech, leaving a ragged hush in its wake. I can hear the receiver rattle in its housing. Everyone stares as the telephone shouts.
“You need to answer it,” Cardner says.
This time Joel does not object. He crosses the distance in silence. He cracks his knuckles and lifts the device.
“What?”
It’s not a question, not really. It sounds more like an accusation. Joel inhales, preparing to continue, and in the space of that breath Stilettos turns to Cardner and smiles. Fuck, does she actually want him? As bad as he wants her?
“You know,” she begins, before letting her voice trail off, the thought hanging mystic in the air.
“No, asshole, I didn’t fucking lie,” Joel lies. “Of course there’s a dead body in here. You sent me a fucking quack and now my friend is a goddamn corpse.”
“You know,” Stilettos reiterates, “there’s a backdoor. To the bank.”
“What?” Cardner asks, speaking with a grating heft, his teeth tearing the gore from a small animal’s bones. “That means--”
He stiffens, abandoning his thought and scanning the room. He stuffs the second pistol in his belt and grips the rifle with both hands.
“Alright, alright,” I catch Joel saying. “Jesus, fuck, yes. My friend shot somebody before he died.”
“Where the fuck is it?” Cardner demands.
He practically rears. I cannot see his gaping nostrils but I can hear the breath beating hard against his mask.
“Behind that support. There’s a hall back there. It’ll take you behind the teller stations, but it’ll also take you outside.”
Stilettos points without looking away, her eyes anything but demure in the face of Cardner’s feral, centaur stamping, 
“Do. Not. Fucking. Move.”
Cardner speaks each word as a sacred, inexorable commandment.
“That’s not my fucking responsibility, Doughnuts,” Joel sneers into the phone.
Fuck. Where the hell do I turn?
Cardner backs away from us, across the lobby and toward the designated doorway. His rifle does not waver. It is his pulpit, the vessel for his long delayed pilgrimage. Joel brays insults with televangelist charisma as Cardner reaches the support--a short brick wall extending out past the furthest teller station--and moves out of sight. Seconds pass. Joel listens to the officer’s response and I hear the click of a door latch.
“Shit!” Cardner’s throat detonates. “Joel! Hang up! Distraction!”
Joel lowers the phone but does not hang up.
“What the fuck?”
“Get. Out!”
From beyond the brick support, Cardner’s rifle appears. A thin black line, aimed slightly upward. Innocuous, maybe not even tangible. An eyelash pressed lightly against a lens, only visible from peripheral views. I blink to make sure it’s really there. Then it fires.
The gun fires and fires and fires. Debris blossoms from the ceiling. Tiles tumble down. More heads appear from the rifle’s hydra muzzle. Nine twinkling crowns. Still more percussions. Words? Gunfire? It shouldn’t matter. Wasn’t I vaccinated against this fear? Why am I so afraid? I crouch. No, I cower. Against the wall. Get smaller get smaller get smaller. I see a scattered pattern of beige and dark blue. I don’t understand. I don’t understand what this is. These freckles before my eyes. The floor? Fuck. I cannot look up. The roaring of my own blood fills my ears. The world collapses. I am a speck. Dirt. Stardust. Suspended in orbit around a titan of fire.
God, what a rush.
“What the fuck are you doing?!”
“Get the fuck out! Get out or someone dies!  Get out get out!”
“Cardner!”
“Joel! Watch the front door!”
“What?”
“Watch the fucking door.”
“I don’t--”
A scuffle.
A long, loud scuffle.
I look up at last. Joel lies on his back. Cardner kneels atop him, kneecap to chest, with the neck of Joel’s shirt wrapped in one cosmic fist. He holds the other above his head, white-knuckled and trembling.
For Christ’s sake, just hatefuck and make up.
“What are we even doing?”
I barely hear the question. But it lingers. Cold, formless. Water grasped by a drowning swimmer or love letters bounced between flickering satellites.
“Fuck you, Cardner. What are you doing?”
Cardner punches down. Joel cocks his head to the side. A gruesome crunch--like a tree snapped by lightning--fills the lobby, even in spite of Cardner’s thick gloves. He does not scream. He simply stares, his face brought even closer to Joel’s.
“You’d better watch the crowd, buddy,” Joel hisses.
Cardner sighs and I wish with all my heart that I could record this. He stands, haltingly, and extends a hand to Joel. Dust and smoke hangs in the air, shrouding the two criminals. Baptising them. My hands shake. I press them against the floor. Stop. Stop. I want more. I push myself up. The Doctor is already standing. The Manager, too.
“What the fuck was that about?” Joel asks, patting his sleeves clean.
“That phone call was a distraction. Guys were coming in from the back.”
“What? How did you know to go look?'
Cardner looks toward Stilettos. She gets to her feet with the Doctor’s help.
“Luck, mostly.”
Joel spits.
“Shit.”
Nobody remains on the floor now. Well, except Terry. And the dead man outside the bathroom, lying spread-eagle on his back, shaping infinite angels in a snow beyond sight.
Cardner moves to speak just as I find my voice. Fuck, hurry. It doesn’t matter whether or not I’m articulate. I stumble through speech and practically yell over Cardner. I remind Joel that, if any of those cops had actually made it inside, they’d likely have pushed either him or Cardner to kill someone. The police gambled with our lives, and if nobody had interrupted the game? They’d have won. Or lost. Or, something. Whatever. We would have lost, and somebody needs to hold them accountable.
“Shut the fuck up,” Cardner commands.
“No,” Joel interrupts, raising a hand. “No, this is a good point.”
He walks with measured grace, his weapon in one hand and my phone in the other. Without a word he presses the pistol to my lips. I open without resisting, receiving the cold metal. It fills me with pride. I stare into the lens of my phone. Make of me a receptacle.
The camera tilts as Joel starts recording.
“This is what the cops want,” he rumbles, pushing the weapon even further into my mouth.
It touches the back of my throat. I fight against gagging and try to breathe through my nose. Beaded droplets of sweat careen down my face. Tears of struggle well in my eyes. My fists tighten. I can picture it perfectly, copies of this brutal moment playing out across countless screens, fueling the appetite of a rapacious country. So many faces taut with rapture, bound by--
The gun leaves and I am empty again. Joel looks down. He types into my phone, adding context to the video, I guess.
Cardner growls. I hear the beginning of a, “Joel,” but the Manager’s wry chuckle cuts through first.
“You boys are sick. I mean, you obviously aren’t good news, but you are so much worse than I thought.”
And then, more to herself than anyone, 
“I am so glad that Perry called in sick today.”
“Perry?” Joel asks, giving unexpected interest to this statement.
“Yeah, my nephew,” she responds, almost instinctively.
The Manager sighs with frustration, probably at herself for engaging Joel’s curiosity, but continues nonetheless.
“I’m glad you aren’t shoving your guns down his throat. No summer job is worth that. That boy does not need any of this.”
Joel smirks. He returns his attention to the phone for just a moment--his little leer shifting as Cardner growls again--and then straightens.
“Lady, I want you to pay real close attention to Perry, the next time you see him. I want you to watch his eyes. See what he does with those brown little soul-windows. I think you might be surprised at what you find. Something like this, it changes everyone it touches.”
“That’s exactly what I meant,” the Manager insists, though her defiance falters, “I’m glad that he didn’t get involved in this.”
Joel’s smirk becomes a smile. For a moment it even flashes.
“Hey,” Cardner barks.
“That’s really nice of you,” Dreadlocks interjects. “Getting your nephew a summer job, I mean. What’s he going to school for?”
The Manager blinks, making no attempt to mask her bewilderment.
“Oh, uh. Perry wants to become an accountant.”
“Hey!” Cardner yells, taking a step forward.
“Oh …”
The trill in Dreadlocks’ voice falters.
“Well, um, maybe you could help him out with that? Y’know, like, intervene. Make sure that he doesn’t become one of the sheep.”
“Excuse me?”
“You know what I mean. Show him that it’s okay to dream. That he doesn’t have to settle. He shouldn’t feel obligated to, I dunno, try to fit into the mold.”
“Hey!”
Everyone finally quiets as Cardner raises his rifle, but his next words contradict all our expectations. I mean, okay. I only know my own expectations. But that’s all that matters. 
Cardner turns to Dreadlocks, his voice concentrated and spiteful.
“You shut up. Just shut right the fuck up, bitch. You don’t know him, and I doubt you know any of them. ‘Sheep.’ Who the fuck do you think you are? How could you possibly understand the courage it takes to live that kind of life? It is hard to bury a dream. Not everyone can choose family over ambition, and then follow through without harboring a secret, fucky bitterness. You know what I mean, people whining about ‘how much better it would have been’ if only they ‘hadn’t settled down.’ Sacrifice is hard. This kid made a tough choice. Show some fucking respect.”
“I just mean--”
“I said shut up. Whatever you think you mean, it’s just a sad little mask for your own pathetic cowardice.”
Dreadlocks’ offended expression serves as her only response. 
“Now, Joel,” Cardner says, turning back toward his accomplice.
But the Manager cuts him off. She thanks him, pauses to consider her words, and then presses on, simultaneously appreciative and condescending.
“That was nice of you, I guess, but I’m still surprised to hear someone like you say something like that.”
She shakes her head.
“I mean, you kids, you’re just so entitled. I guess you figure that if you can’t get the government to pay for your front facing camera, you’ll just outright steal other people's money. I guess you think you’re cutting out the middleman.”
“Hey,” Joel cuts in, before Cardner can respond. “Not everything has to be about money, lady. You want happy kids? Give ‘em fewer things and more experiences.”
“Are you fucking kidding?” Buddy scoffs.
Joel smiles, but before he can speak again I blurt out what I know he must be thinking. Not about Buddy’s comment--who gives a fuck if Joel’s being a hypocrite?--but about money and experiences and value.
It feels good, this certainty. I won’t lie: I’ve been pretty desperate to learn what comes next in the sacrament. I even started to consider that maybe nothing remained. I’ve felt the end approaching, and I really did suspect that I’d fulfilled the entirety of my role. I mean, I could always encourage more of the same, but armies are meant to advance. And lo, I got what I wanted. The gods reward the faithful.
Anyway, yeah, I tell Joel that he should auction off some of his hostage’s belongings.
“Holy shit.”
Joel directs this remark toward Cardner.
“We can come out ahead after all.”
“Like hell, Joel.”
But distraction has once again overtaken Joel. He looks between each of his hostages, one after the other, his eyes roving from bottom to top, critical and carnivorous. Cardner steps in front of him, ostensibly a protective gesture, though I know he isn’t protecting any of us. I contemplate this for a moment and realize, holy shit, I don’t think he’s ever turned his back before now.
“Joel, listen to me. Seriously, listen. I’m not going to say this again. Whatever the hell you think you’re doing, you need to knock it off. This isn’t helping. You are going to fuck this up. I’m sorry about ...” Terry ... “everything, but we can deal with that later. Right now I need you to stop and think. I need you to work with me. I need to get out of here. I want you to get out, too, but I need to. I am not negotiating. I am not giving you a choice, Joel. You need to work with me.”
I can almost feel the cliff on which I’m standing, feel the tips of my shoes jutting out into open air. If I move, if I speak, if I breathe, I can’t really know how it will affect what comes next. Dammit. Dammit. I fucking hate this. Come on, Joel!
Joel does not hesitate long. He pushes wordlessly past Cardner, hunching, not meeting his partner’s eyes. I watch Cardner deflate, almost literally. He shrinks, diminishing as he turns, and presses himself back against the wall. No, I spoke too soon. He doesn’t get smaller, not exactly. He just … turns. I can still sense the weight of his presence, but it’s like he’s around a corner or something. He’s a station on a poorly tuned radio.
Joel slinks up to Wax Job with lips curled back, the grin of a savage dog.
“Give me your vest.”
“What?”
“Give me your fucking vest, asshole.”
“What? No. You can’t just--”
“Really? I can’t take your vest? We’re in the middle of a fucking bank robbery, and I can’t fucking take your vest? Try again, Professor Cockgoblin.”
Wax Job rolls his eyes. He begins to remove his vest, but Joel stops him.
“No, wait. Let me get a picture of you wearing it first.”
Joel backs up, holding out my phone. Wax Job wears confusion, along with the half-removed vest.
“No, Jesus. Put it back on. You--well, okay, that’s better than nothing. Seriously, though. Are you just gonna stand there like that? Someone like you, I’d have figured this wasn’t your first time playing dressup.”
Wax Job glowers as Joel snaps a picture.
“I guess this’ll have to do. Now hand it over. No, leave your name tag where it is.”
Joel grabs the vest and tosses it onto the nearest teller station. He moves toward Dreadlocks, taking from her a crystal necklace. He takes Stilettos’s shoes, Pinot Grigio’s cardigan, RBF’s belt. Joel moves toward Buddy Holly, pointing at his glasses.
“What? No way. You can’t take my glasses. I need these.”
Joel doesn’t respond. Buddy blinks, swallows, sighs. He removes his glasses and tosses them to Joel.
“Okay, so. If you’re going to sell off our stuff, can you at least get us something to eat?” Dreadlocks asks. “We’ve been in here basically forever. It’s a billion degrees. I’m starting to get a food headache. Can’t you call the police and have them send over some food?”
“What? No.”
Joel’s sarcastic playfulness leaves abruptly. He looks toward Terry’s body, and then toward the hallway leading back behind the teller stations.
“No, I’m not going to talk to the cops anymore.”
“Wait, what?”
Dreadlocks recoils.
“Are you serious? What are you even doing, then?”
“I’m doing what has to be done.”
Joel returns his attention to the phone.
“It doesn’t fucking seem that way,” RBF observes “It seems like you are just doing whatever the fuck you want.”
A warband settles on Joel’s brow.
“I am doing what has to be done.”
“Really, though, are you sure?” Stilettos asks. “What ‘need’ you are satisfying? For whose interests are you providing?”
“Think about it,” the Doctor adds, “what are you holding onto? You murdered a man in cold blood. Your friend was killed in the process. Isn’t that enough? What is worth that price?”
“No.”
Joel throws out his hand, surveying the entire topography of accusation. His pointed finger brings his target into focus.
“Don’t fucking talk about Terry. Don’t you dare. What do you even know? What do you know about any of this? I am doing what needs to be done.”
“She has a point, though” Wax Job insists. “What are you doing? You boys are obviously smart. Or at least, smarter than most superficial judgements would suggest. So what are you doing? This is the twenty-first century. You don’t rob a bank like this. This isn’t The Untouchables. You don’t need this.”
“Smart?”
Joel laughs, longer than he has all day. His body convulses with tensioned tremors. He doubles over and clutches his sides, spilling disingenuous mirth out onto the sweltering floor of the bank.
Eventually, awkwardly, he straightens and clears his eyes.
“You think we’re smart? Oh no. Oh no no no no no. We are not smart. That is the craziest damn thing I’ve heard all day. And pal, it’s been one hell of a day. No, we are not smart. We are idiots. We are terminal fucking morons. All of us! You are, too. And her, and him,” Joel points through the crowd at no one in particular, “and her too. All of us. We bought into the longest, cruelest con ever set in motion. We signed up for it, in fact. Eager. Desperate. We were told that things mattered. We assumed that we understood. We were deceived.”
“So it really is about doing what you want,” the Manager interjects. “Use whatever cute language you think will help, but it still sounds like you boys decided to rob a bank so you could get what you wanted.”
“We shouldn’t have to pay for the shortcomings in your life,” Pinot Grigio agrees, her voice barely above a whisper.
“This is not about what we want! Except that it is, I suppose. But it’s not about what you think we want. It’s about what we need to want. In the face of a greater and unattainable want. In the face of an heirloom lie.”
Joel backs away. He spreads his arms, pulling the entire room into an airy embrace. His body continues to grow. He is expansive. Every subsequent gesture fills the broken, circumambient void.
“They told us to follow our dreams. They told us to work hard. They said that, if we only believed in our blood and sweat, we could be whatever we wanted to be. And God, how we wanted to believe! How we wanted to find value in effort, to find meaning in it. To sieve merit from all this frightening, ubiquitous chaos. We yearned with all the strained passion that our little bodies could muster. We sat awake at night, reaching and reaching and reaching. We followed the rules. We tried our hardest. Because why would they lie? We walked across that stage to so much applause and grasped unknowingly the trumpets of judgement.”
“But you aren’t entitled to happiness,” Buddy chimes.
“Yeah,” Dreadlocks adds. “You have to find it all on your own. Chase down your dreams!”
“Well thank God. Thank heaven above, ladies and gents, ‘cause I’ve found at last the warden of dreams. So you’re the one who gets to decide, then? You call the shots? Then tell me, gatekeeper. Where do you draw that line? The one that separates a valid dream from a joke. Tell me what distinguishes a lauded ambition from a bitterly mocked conceit. I mean, it’s a shitty verdict to enforce--and I don’t really see the point--but apparently somebody does. Because it keeps on fucking happening.”
“I,” and Dreadlocks pauses for longer than an ellipsis, “don’t understand.”
“Yes you do. Yes you do!”
Joel jumps without warning. He grabs hold of the nearest counter and heaves himself onto a teller station. Paper pitches through the air. A computer monitor crashes to the floor. His head tilts back. He holds his fists above his eyes. The gun. The phone. He yells. I can see the sound pass up through his throat like a breaching leviathan. He levels his gaze, his face soaked with perspiration or tears or both.
Just let me take a picture, please.
“Listen. We didn’t ask for this. And I don’t mean Cardner and Terry and I, and I don’t just mean this botched fucking robbery. I mean all of us, in everything. Every fucking kid with a front facing camera. None of us asked for any of this. Of all the constructs for which we are mocked, we we built none of them. They were given to us. Consolation trophies. Right. Like the one buried in Terry’s stomach. We inherited a world that was designed to nurture our guilt. What a good job it does, too. And not just by artifice! We are guilty, no matter what we do. This is our lineage and our legacy. We are fated. An entire generation, never more at home than when we are lost. Humiliated for enduring the very struggles forced upon us.
“And you all want to tell me that I’m wrong. That this isn’t how it has to be. Well fuck you too. What else is there? When our promised future is bound in iron, what is left for us but to do as we want? Look into my heart, any one of you, and show me which artery separates need from desire.”
I hear a quiet shuffle. The room capsizes. The Doctor stands opposite Joel, aiming a gun squarely at his chest.
What.
What.
Cardner has his rifle. Cardner has one pistol. No, Cardner has two pistols. Joel holds the fourth gun for everyone to see. No more armaments were brought into the bank.
Except.
The dead man.
He lies on his back, his final secret released to the sky.
“Come on.”
The Doctor speaks softly.
“Come on down, Joel. I’m sorry about what happened to your friend. I really am. But it’s time to come down. Put your gun away. You have to let go.”
Joel says nothing. He mirrors her stance. He aims his weapon at her.
“What are you doing, Joel?” the Doctor continues, utterly undaunted. “You don’t want to kill anyone. There has been enough death already. You know that. Come down. You don’t want to kill anyone.”
Joel swallows. He responds quietly, like the hum of the Earth twirling through space.
“What is left but for me to do as I want?”
He tenses the muscles in his arm. He exhales. No creature alive breathes in that moment.
I feel the gunshot through every particle of my being.
Joel staggers. He throws his arms outward and does not fall. He coughs. He looks down toward the small hole in his chest. Blood. He looks up toward Cardner.
“I’m sorry,” Cardner says, his gun held far out before him.
Cardner brings his shaking sidearm to his face. He pulls his mask upward, revealing the eyes of an abandoned dog. He repeats his sorrow. Quieter.
“I’m sorry.”
Joel shakes his head.
“No.”
Joel drops his weapon. It falls like a meteor. 
He smiles. He holds up the phone. His hand quivers. He holds the phone higher. He freezes in apotheosis. For just quick a moment. Then his eyes dim and he topples forward. The phone spins away.
I chase down the device before it skids to a halt. Nobody else moves. I pick it up. I hold it close. I check that final picture.
I see a blur. A formless smear of color. An abstract panorama of this private cosmos.
I tap my thumb and post it.
